 gospel of  the kingdom it mainly was focused on  them as a  and how they're greater than everyone  else particularly greater than  mainstream Christianity they were  constantly criticizing mainstream  Christianity and how there's so much  better like look we're living exactly  the way that says we should in the book  of Acts you know they're quoting these  Bible verses to me in Spanish see look  this is how you're supposed to live  following the Bible I have this thought  in my mind like well what if everyone  lived like this you know it'd be the  greatest thing if everyone on earth was  like this it could be peaceful everyone  would have a purpose I thought it was  great and I was like I want to find out  what happens if everyone lives like this  I want to do my part to make that happen  and so within three weeks I was baptized  so I became a member of the community  for the most part I was pretty happy  there for a while until I had suppressed  these urges that all young men have you  know in their minds all those feelings  started coming back at a certain point I  went to this man that they had appointed  as the Shepherd over me and I went to  him and I told him about these thoughts  I was having and he said that you got a  masturbate but you can't think about sex  while you're doing it you got to have  just a mechanical release is what they  called it and then the next problem I  ran into was they have what they call  the home teaching it's based on Genesis  9 Noah has three sons with them  Noah got drunk and one of his son Tom  was disrespectful vanilla so Noah cursed  Tom and said that his descendants shall  be the slaves of sins descendeth Shem  was the other son who was you know  respectful so they teach that all  shemites all descendants of Shem are are  white people so all black people are  descendants of Tom  so they teach that actually race-based  slavery was ordained by God I heard this  about a year after being there so like I  was already fully indoctrinated I don't  know I wanted everything else they said  true but it was really a struggle for me  to hear this you know my dad's black and  they they consider me to be a homo  because it's based on your father's  lineage that was a real struggle for me  another thing they tried to explain to  me was essentially by generalizing how  they felt all black people live they  said well wouldn't wouldn't they be  better off here  you know wouldn't they be better off  being humble and submitting to Yahshua  the Messiah you can't even get married  unless they say you can't and and then  they'll tell you who you can marry it's  completely up to them that your whole  life is just in their hands you know I  continued to to try my best but  eventually I came to a point where I  just I didn't believe it anymore  so the woman who's now my wife she was  living on the in the same community as  me and Missouri she gave me this little  note and you're not supposed to talk to  each other if you're single and of the  opposite sex outside of public setting I  initiated things I approached her and  she writes me this note and that's how  we got caught I don't think I would have  approached her had I not had those  doubts in my head in the first place I  had seen that she was she was struggling  to like she wanted to leave - and she  was ready to hitchhike I was like you  don't have to do that just you know we  can leave together yeah they had a  meeting they kicked us out and that  night we were on a bus for northern  Michigan we had to go live with my  stepdad on his farm we got to northern  Michigan in November of 2012 and it was  already getting cold and that winter it  was just really a harsh winter  eventually I did find a job at a local  farm in the springtime and I worked for  a few years and we had a son we named  him Terrance and eventually I went back  to college and I got a bookkeeping  certificate while my wife worked and I  got a job as an administrative assistant  in a war